another wave of mass protests in the  sudanese capital khartoum on thursday  thousands took to the streets to express  their anger against the military which  took over power last  when year take to the streets every day  and people die every day it's not for  nothing it's for change it's for a  civilian government a democratic  government that protects the right of  people  it is our right to protest the military  will not rule us this country is  supposed to be a civilian democratic  country  protesters are also out against the  former ruling party led by president  ahmad bashir who was ousted by the  military in april 2019.  thursday marked 33 years since the coup  that he staged to grab power his party  has been disbanded after his overthrow  but protesters say the loyalists are  still in charge  the military in power now are members of  bashar's security committee all they did  was replace the figures but the former  ruling party are still in key positions  in government institutions nothing has  changed and that's why we're out  protesting we're out to overthrow the  security committee and the former ruling  party  security forces used tear gas and live  ammunition to disperse protesters  several were killed and hundreds injured  more than 100 people have died in  anti-military demonstrations the  military has repeatedly stated it will  not step down unless elections are held  or a political consensus is reached  reaching a political consensus has so  far proven to be difficult talks between  political parties facilitated by the  united nations african union and the  regional igad were boricotted by  pro-democracy groups and later suspended  and no new date has been set for its  resumption  political parties have refused to join  the talks saying they want to end the  military takeover and not enter into a  power sharing agreement  a political solution if it achieves the  aims of the street then we can be a part  of it the political process in its  current form is something we put  objective conditions on if they're not  met we can't go forward the most  important condition is the aim of the  political process if it's ending the  military takeover we can talk about the  ways otherwise we can't go ahead  but the military continues to hold on to  power provoking chaos on the streets and  hopes her transition to democracy fades  further hebel morgan al jazeera hartum
 

